  "overview": "The BeagleBone Black runs full Linux like a Raspberry Pi, but its standout feature is something a Pi doesn't have at all: two onboard PRUs (Programmable Realtime Units) — tiny independent co-processors that can bit-bang precise, jitter-free timing (step-motor pulses, custom protocols) completely outside of Linux's non-real-time scheduling. It also boots from onboard eMMC storage out of the box, no SD card required, and exposes far more pins than a Pi across two 46-pin headers (P8 and P9) instead of one 40-pin header. The pin naming convention (P9_11, P8_7, etc.) refers to physical header position and is its own thing to learn, distinct from both Pi's GPIO numbers and Arduino's D-numbers.",
  "simpleSummary": "A Linux mini-computer like a Raspberry Pi, with extra chips built in for very precise timing.",
  "specifications": {
    "processor": "TI Sitara AM3358, Arm Cortex-A8, 1 GHz",
    "operatingVoltage": "5V (board), 3.3V (GPIO logic)",
    "ram": "512 MB DDR3",
    "storage": "4 GB onboard eMMC, plus a microSD slot",
    "realTimeCoprocessors": "2x PRU (Programmable Realtime Unit) for hard real-time I/O",
    "connectivity": "10/100 Ethernet",
    "usb": "Mini-USB (client/power) + USB-A (host)",
    "video": "Micro-HDMI",
    "gpio": "2x 46-pin headers (P8, P9), 3.3V logic",
    "connector": "Mini-USB (power) or 5V barrel jack"
  },
